@prefix tmk:     <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/> .
@prefix tmkc:    <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/concept/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/examination>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "TM-Knowledge — examination module"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Who does what, and to what.

**No longer entirely unpopulated, and the change is worth stating precisely.**
Until 2026-09-12 this module said that *"the approved set describes what the
Manual says about section 43, not a process model of examination, and inventing
one would be authoring practice"*. The first half is still true of the
**approved** set and always will be — all 35 signed relationships are sourced
to `TMM/Part29`. The second half described a prohibition ADR-0079 removed on
2026-09-08: authoring practice, stamped and evidenced, is the job.

What now stands behind these classes is content, not a proposal. The
vocabulary holds 21 concepts typed `procedural_step` and 8 typed
`process_role` (ADR-0098), and `authored/relationships.yaml` holds 31
`isPerformedBy` edges joining the second to the first across the whole Manual
— acceptance to the examiner, revocation of acceptance to the Registrar,
opposition to the opponent. **Every one of those was written by a machine and
read by nobody** (ADR-0109, ADR-0110).

The role classes were declared empty to make a gap visible. Five of the nine
terms the reviewer's covering note named
(`review/returned/260826-expert-feedback.md`, HANDOFF Q17) now have a concept
record behind them and **four still do not** — *Office Practice*, *Subject
Matter Expert*, *Adverse Report* and *Team Leader* are absent from both stores.
Q-28's cause is Q-53: a term's definition is usually not in a passage that
cites anything, so no citation-based rule reaches them, and the boundary that
was blamed for this in the first place is gone."""@en-AU ;
    owl:imports <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/legal-concepts> ;
    owl:versionInfo "draft — not approved" .

tmk:TradeMarkApplication a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Trade mark application"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED. The thing being examined. No application data exists in this programme and none is in scope."@en-AU .

tmk:Examination a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Examination"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED. The process applied to an application."@en-AU .

tmk:Objection a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Objection"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED. A ground raised against an application in an examination."@en-AU .

tmk:ExaminationOutcome a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Examination outcome"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """UNPOPULATED, and it must stay that way for this pilot.

Deciding an examination outcome is explicitly out of scope (CLAUDE.md §6), and
the prohibited-use set exists to keep it there: PU-0001 and PU-0002 are the two
outputs that state one."""@en-AU .

# --- roles: declared, undefined, and the gap is the point -------------------

tmk:Role a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Role"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A person or office acting in the examination system."@en-AU .

tmk:Registrar a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Registrar"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNDEFINED as a class, but the vocabulary now carries the term twice — GC-0046 signed, GC-0115 authored — and that duplication is one of the ten ADR-0101 put to the expert rather than something to tidy away. Load-bearing here: the Manual states practice and does not bind this office's discretion."@en-AU .

tmk:Delegate a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Delegate"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNDEFINED — HANDOFF Q17. The word exists in the vocabulary only inside GC-0046's alt label «the Registrar's delegate», which is not the same as holding the term: nothing can say that a delegate decided something."@en-AU .

tmk:Examiner a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Examiner"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNDEFINED as a class. GC-0044 carries the term and 7 of the 31 authored isPerformedBy edges name it as the office that takes a step — the Registrar takes 13 of the rest, the applicant 9. Every one of them is unreviewed, and the applicant's 9 are the ones to distrust: an applicant more often has a step done to them than does one."@en-AU .

tmk:DecisionMaker a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Decision maker"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """UNDEFINED — HANDOFF Q17, and the reviewer corrected two records on precisely
this point (GE-0010, GE-0047: decision maker vs the Registrar).

It matters for Q16: the office's bar for applying the presumption of
registrability is not that the individual examiner doubts a connotation exists,
but that the Registrar as a whole does. A model that equates the two roles
loses that distinction silently."""@en-AU .

tmk:SubjectMatterExpert a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Subject matter expert"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNDEFINED, and the term is ABSENT FROM BOTH STORES — one of the four of the reviewer's nine that the whole-Manual concept pass still did not reach (Q-53). The expert request asks for it in prose rather than guessing at a record."@en-AU .

tmk:TeamLeader a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Team leader"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNDEFINED, and ABSENT FROM BOTH STORES. Named in the reviewer's note as part of the consultation the office expects before a connotation is accepted on the basis of doubt (Q16), and the Manual names a «Examination team leader» in Part 38 — so the corpus has the words and the vocabulary has not taken them."@en-AU .

tmk:decidedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "decided by"@en-AU .

tmk:constrainsRole a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Role ;
    rdfs:label "constrains role"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a proposition to the role whose conduct it governs. The hook Q16's rule about examiner conduct would attach to — no record type currently holds such a rule, and whether one should is an expert-and-owner call."@en-AU .

# --- what the authored edges now attach to ----------------------------------
#
# AUTHORED 2026-09-12, read by nobody (ADR-0110).
#
# `tmk:isPerformedBy` is declared in relations.ttl, where it is generated from
# the records that use it and typed tmk:AuthoredRelation. What it needed here
# was somewhere for its two ends to mean something: a step is a
# tmk:ProceduralStep in the concept vocabulary, an office is a
# tmk:ProcessRole, and NEITHER of those is tmk:Role.
#
# That distinction is the one legal-concepts.ttl already draws and it is the
# reason this comment exists. A tmk:Role is somebody who acts — an examiner who
# signs a report. A tmk:ProcessRole is the *term* "examiner" as an entry in the
# vocabulary. Every authored isPerformedBy edge joins two vocabulary entries,
# so it runs ProceduralStep to ProcessRole, and nothing in this repo has ever
# asserted anything about a tmk:Role instance because no application data
# exists. Merging the two would make the concept record for examiner an
# examiner, and Q-58 is the note that saw the collision coming.

tmk:ProcessRole rdfs:seeAlso tmk:Role .

tmk:performsInPractice a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "performs in practice"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "AUTHORED, unreviewed, and deliberately an annotation so it licenses no inference. The bridge a later pass would need between the vocabulary's tmk:ProcessRole entry and the tmk:Role that acts. Declared and UNUSED: asserting it today would join a term to an office on nothing but the two having the same label, which is the inference Q-58 exists to prevent."@en-AU .
